            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Multiply the probability of one red–white–green order by 6, since all six orders have the same probability.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let $R$, $W$ and $G$ represent red, white and green. The six favourable orders are $RWG$, $RGW$, $WRG$, $WGR$, $GRW$ and $GWR$. Replacement keeps the colour probabilities unchanged, so these orders have equal probability.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{all different colours})&=6\\times\\frac{3}{12}\\times\\frac{4}{12}\\times\\frac{5}{12}\\\\P(\\text{all different colours})&=\\frac{5}{24}\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
